<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 22</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 22 \u00b7 Structure &amp; Exterior</div><h1>Metal Roofing vs Architectural Shingles</h1><p>Compare appearance, durability, pitch compatibility and installed system cost.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Architectural-shingle roof system</td><td>$4.50\u2013$6.75</td><td>per roof SF total, standard roof already included</td></tr><tr><td>Metal roofing system</td><td>$6.50\u2013$10.00</td><td>per roof SF total; NOT incremental upgrade</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section><section class=\"asi-option-mini-gallery\"><h2>Visual comparison \u2014 planned photographs</h2><p>Images will be added where they help explain actual differences. No placeholder is represented as an installed product or completed ASI project.</p><div class=\"asi-mini-grid\"><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Overall appearance</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Close-up detail</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Installed scale / context</div></div></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>This comparison is designed to help homeowners understand what changes when they move from a conventional architectural shingle roof to corrugated metal, exposed-fastener metal, heavier-gauge metal, or standing seam.</p><p>The right roof should match the purpose of the structure, the look of the property, the expected ownership period, and the level of long-term maintenance you are comfortable with.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>The essentials</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>This comparison is designed to help homeowners understand what changes when they move from a conventional architectural shingle roof to corrugated metal, exposed-fastener metal, heavier-gauge metal, or standing seam.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The right roof should match the purpose of the structure, the look of the property, the expected ownership period, and the level of long-term maintenance you are comfortable with.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>Which Roof Option Makes the Most Sense?</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>For a basic storage shed, architectural shingles are usually the most cost-effective roof. For a long-term workshop, studio, backyard office, pool house, garden building, or highly visible premium structure, metal roofing often provides stronger long-term value because it can last longer, shed weather well, and reduce future replacement risk.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>The cheapest roof on installation day is not always the lowest-cost roof over time.</h3><p>Shingles usually cost less upfront. Metal roofing usually becomes more attractive when the building is expected to stay on the property for decades or when the structure is finished, highly visible, or used often.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>Start With How the Building Will Be Used.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Most homeowners are not really shopping for a roof gauge or a product label. They are trying to solve a practical problem: lower cost, less maintenance, better appearance, better storm performance, or a roof that looks right beside the home.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Architectural Shingles</h3><p>Best when budget matters most and the building is mainly used for storage, garden tools, or seasonal equipment.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Architectural Shingles</h3><p>Best when the goal is to visually coordinate with a home, garage, or neighborhood where shingles are the expected residential look.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Corrugated Metal</h3><p>Best when the structure is intended to feel barn-inspired, rustic, agricultural, or Appalachian in character.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>26ga Exposed-Fastener Metal</h3><p>Best for long-term ownership when you want a stronger metal roof without moving into the premium standing seam price range.</p><p>Best when the building will see regular use and the owner wants a stronger roof system with better long-term durability.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-4\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 04</div><h2>How Each Roof System Performs Over Time</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Every roof system has strengths, tradeoffs, and ideal use cases. These planning ranges are not guarantees. Actual performance depends on material quality, installation, roof pitch, ventilation, tree cover, storm exposure, maintenance, and site conditions.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Architectural Shingles</h3><p>Planning Lifespan: approximately 18\u201328 years in typical Ohio Valley conditions.</p><p>Best For: budget-friendly builds, traditional home-like appearance, and short-to-mid-term ownership.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Corrugated Metal Roofing</h3><p>Planning Lifespan: approximately 25\u201340 years depending on panel quality, coating, installation, and maintenance.</p><p>Best For: utility sheds, barns, rustic structures, agricultural-style buildings, and informal work buildings.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>29ga Exposed-Fastener Metal</h3><p>Best For: standard sheds, workshops, utility buildings, and residential/light commercial backyard structures.</p><p>Provides many of the weather-shedding benefits of metal roofing at a more accessible price.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>26ga Standing Seam Metal</h3><p>Planning Lifespan: approximately 40\u201370+ years depending on material, finish, installation, roof design, and maintenance.</p><p>Best For: backyard offices, studios, heated/cooled spaces, pool houses, high-visibility structures, and premium architectural buildings.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-5\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 05</div><h2>Installed Cost Comparison \u2014 10\u00d712 Backyard Building Example</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Architectural shingles serve as the baseline. The examples below use a practical 10\u00d712 backyard building roof-area assumption of approximately 135 square feet of roof surface, not 120 square feet of floor area. Actual roof area changes with pitch, overhangs, porch roofs, dormers, valleys, and model design.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Planning note: These are educational planning ranges, not final contract pricing. Final roof cost may vary based on roof pitch, overhangs, ridge details, valleys, underlayment, trim, fasteners, color selection, labor, access, model design, current material pricing, and whether the roof is part of a larger building package.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-6\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 06</div><h2>Cost Per Year of Ownership \u2014 A Better Comparison</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The lowest upfront roof is not always the lowest lifetime roof. A roof that lasts longer can become more attractive over time, even when it costs more on installation day.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Cost-per-year figures are simplified planning estimates based on example installed cost divided by the planning lifespan range. They do not include future repairs, maintenance, color changes, storm damage, insurance factors, replacement labor, or changes in material pricing.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-7\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 07</div><h2>The Maintenance Difference Most Buyers Overlook</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Every roof system has a tradeoff. A trustworthy roof comparison should explain the advantages and the maintenance realities, not just the selling points.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Shingle Maintenance</h3><p>Architectural shingles are familiar, quiet, and cost-effective, but they can lose granules, curl, lift, or need isolated replacement after storms. Heat, tree cover, roof pitch, and ventilation all influence how long they last.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exposed-Fastener Metal</h3><p>Exposed-fastener metal roofs perform well and offer strong value, but the screws and washers remain visible and should be inspected over time. Temperature movement can eventually create fastener maintenance needs.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Standing Seam Metal</h3><p>Standing seam is the premium metal option because the fasteners are concealed. That cleaner design reduces exposed-fastener concerns and gives the building a more architectural, residential-grade appearance.</p><p>Metal roofing can be more noticeable during rain, especially on an unfinished storage building. For offices, studios, or heated/cooled spaces, insulation and ceiling assemblies can significantly improve sound comfort.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Resale Appearance</h3><p>A premium roof can make a backyard building look more intentional and permanent. Standing seam and heavier-gauge metal tend to feel more architectural, while shingles feel familiar and traditional.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-8\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 08</div><h2>Why Local Weather Changes the Roof Decision</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Roof selection in Greater Cincinnati, Northern Kentucky, and Southeast Indiana is not only about appearance. Humidity, freeze-thaw cycles, summer heat, storm rain, wind, tree debris, and seasonal temperature swings all affect long-term roof performance.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Heavy rain: metal roofs shed water quickly, while shingle performance depends heavily on pitch, flashing, and condition.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Humidity: airflow, ventilation, and moisture management matter for any backyard structure.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>A backyard roof should be selected for Ohio Valley weather, not just showroom appearance.</h3><p>In this region, roof decisions should consider more than color. The best choice accounts for rain shedding, fastener exposure, maintenance access, nearby trees, sun exposure, and how finished the building will be inside.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-9\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 09</div><h2>Choose the Roof That Matches the Purpose of the Building.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A roof should not be upgraded just to spend more money. It should solve a real ownership issue: longer life, better appearance, lower maintenance, stronger weather performance, or a more finished architectural result.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>For a basic storage shed, architectural shingles remain a sensible and economical choice. They look familiar, keep upfront cost down, and perform well when properly installed on the right building.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>For a premium shed, workshop, garden building, or long-term backyard structure, metal roofing is often the stronger ownership choice. If the goal is the best balance of durability and price, 26ga exposed-fastener metal is the practical sweet spot.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-10\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 10</div><h2>Straight Answers Before You Choose a Roof</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These are the questions homeowners usually ask when comparing shingles, corrugated metal, exposed-fastener metal, and standing seam metal for a backyard structure.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-11\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 11</div><h2>Compare Roof Options During Your Build Planning.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The right roof should match the purpose of the building, the look of the property, your long-term ownership plan, and the level of maintenance you are willing to accept. We can review the practical choices with you before your final build plan is approved.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section verified-planning\" id=\"verified-planning\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">CURRENT PLANNING GUIDANCE</div><h2>Compare complete assemblies, not simply roof covering materials</h2><p>Traditional ASI models generally have architectural asphalt shingles in the standard exterior-shell scope; metal roofing, if available for a particular model, must be quoted as a separate engineered and detailed assembly.</p><p>At 2/12, both asphalt-shingle and metal systems require product-specific minimum-pitch checks and approved underlayment and flashing. A generic claim that one roof material is always suitable would be misleading.</p><p>Technical references: <a href=\"https://www.asphaltroofing.org/installation-of-asphalt-shingles-on-lower-sloped-roofs/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ARMA: low-slope shingle installation</a></p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Compare your preferred roofline with the actual model design, building height, roof-material requirements and applicable local restrictions before requesting a revised written proposal.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"references\"><h2>Relevant planning references</h2><p>These official and manufacturer resources provide additional background. Product and jurisdiction requirements must be checked for the exact project.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.asphaltroofing.org/installation-of-asphalt-shingles-on-lower-sloped-roofs/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Asphalt Roofing Manufacturers Association: low-slope installation</a></p></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#verified-planning\">Current planning guidance</a><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">The essentials</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">Which Roof Option Makes the Most Sense?</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">Start With How the Building Will Be Used.</a><a href=\"#topic-4\">How Each Roof System Performs Over Time</a><a href=\"#topic-5\">Installed Cost Comparison \u2014 10\u00d712 Backyard Building Example</a><a href=\"#topic-6\">Cost Per Year of Ownership \u2014 A Better Comparison</a><a href=\"#topic-7\">The Maintenance Difference Most Buyers Overlook</a><a href=\"#topic-8\">Why Local Weather Changes the Roof Decision</a><a href=\"#topic-9\">Choose the Roof That Matches the Purpose of the Building.</a><a href=\"#topic-10\">Straight Answers Before You Choose a Roof</a><a href=\"#topic-11\">Compare Roof Options During Your Build Planning.</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a href=\"#references\">Planning references</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

